prompt_md:
Ledgr is a banking app with three tiers: the mobile app, Ledgr's app servers and a database. Right now the app servers hold the database login and check the rule "no transfer larger than the account balance". The database has no constraint, permission or stored procedure that enforces this rule.

To save time, a team suggests removing the app servers. Under this proposal:

- every copy of the Ledgr mobile app would connect to the database with **one shared database login stored inside the app**;
- the app would send its own SQL queries straight to the database, naming the tables directly;
- the transfer rule would be checked **only in the mobile app's code**;
- there would be no compatibility layer (such as a view under an old table name) between the app and the tables.

Under these assumptions, which statements about the two-tier design are true? Select all that apply.
